{
  "dom_id": "step-q113771",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "For the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}+1){e}^{x},$ defined on the closed interval $[-2,0],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The global minimum value is $\\frac{5}{{e}^{2}}$\n\nII. The global minimum value is $\\frac{2}{e}$\n\nIII. The global maximum value is $1$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=({x}^{2}+1){e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x{e}^{x}+({x}^{2}+1){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{x} \\\\  & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}{e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-1,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,0$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =((-2)^{2}+1){e}^{-2}=\\frac{5}{{e}^{2}}≈0.68 \\\\ f(-1) & =((-1)^{2}+1){e}^{-1}=\\frac{2}{e}≈0.74 \\\\ f(0) & =({0}^{2}+1){e}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=1,$ so the global maximum is $1.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-2)=\\frac{5}{{e}^{2}},$ so the global minimum is $\\frac{5}{{e}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14150956/assets/8768b85a24b4351a481fec2801be274990b2e524a7322bfcf07b257884b9409f.png)",
